.Dd August 20, 2026
.Dt CAIRND 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm cairnd
.Nd serve ZIM archives over a local HTTP API
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl c Ar file
.Op Fl -check
.Op Fl V
.Op Fl h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
opens every
.Pa *.zim
archive in a directory, binds a local socket, confines itself, and serves the
API described in
.Xr cairn-api 7 .
It never writes to an archive and holds no write capability to any path once
initialization is over.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width "-c file"
.It Fl c , Fl -config Ar file
Configuration file.
Without this,
.Pa /etc/cairn/cairn.conf
is used if it exists, and built-in defaults otherwise.
An explicitly named file must exist.
.It Fl -check
Parse the configuration, open every archive, check that the listener could be
bound, report what was found, and exit.
Nothing is bound and nothing is served.
Use it before restarting a running daemon.
.It Fl V , Fl -version
Print the version and exit.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print usage and exit.
.El
.Sh STARTUP ORDER
The order is the security property, not an implementation detail:
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
Archives are opened and mapped, while the process can still open files.
An unreadable or malformed archive, or two archives sharing a UUID, is a
startup error naming the files.
.It
The listener is bound, while the process can still bind.
.It
Worker threads are created and parked, while the process can still create
threads.
.It
The process confines itself
.Pq Sx CONFINEMENT .
.It
The workers are released.
The first request is served by an already-confined process.
.El
.Sh CONFINEMENT
Three independent layers, none assuming another is present.
What was applied is reported by
.Pa /v1/status
and by
.Ql cairn status ,
because a daemon that failed to confine itself otherwise looks identical to one
that succeeded.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy no_new_privs
Set first; seccomp requires it and Landlock relies on it.
.It Sy Landlock
Read-only access to the archive directory and nothing else.
Every filesystem right the running kernel's ABI knows about is handled and
then not granted, so no path outside that directory is reachable and nothing
anywhere is writable.
On ABI 4 and later, network access is denied outright as well.
.It Sy seccomp
An allowlist for the serving loop, installed on every thread.
.Ql clone ,
.Ql execve ,
.Ql socket ,
.Ql connect
and
.Ql bind
are absent: archives are already mapped, workers already exist, and the
listener is already bound.
A syscall outside the list kills the process by default; see
.Ql sandbox_action
in
.Xr cairn.conf 5
for the alternatives.
.Pp
The open family
.Pq Ql open , Ql openat , Ql openat2
is refused rather than fatal: the call always fails with
.Er EACCES ,
so no path can be opened, but a library that probes the filesystem for a
tunable gets an error instead of taking the daemon with it.
glibc's allocator does exactly that \(em it reads
.Pa /proc/sys/vm/overcommit_memory
when it grows a second arena's heap, and
.Pa /sys/devices/system/cpu/online
when it counts processors \(em and it does so the first time the workload
happens to need it, which may be under load rather than at startup.
.El
.Pp
.Ql sandbox require
turns a partially applied sandbox into a refusal to start.
Use it once you know the kernel supports what you asked for;
.Ql cairn status
tells you.
.Sh DEPLOYMENT CONSTRAINTS
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Sy Archives are immutable once opened .
They are mapped, not read through
.Xr pread 2 ,
which is the right call for multi-gigabyte files.
The consequence is accepted explicitly: if an archive is truncated or replaced
in place under a running daemon, a load faults with
.Dv SIGBUS ,
which is not recoverable in safe Rust, and
.Nm
does not attempt to catch it.
In practice a write whose source pages lost their backing fails with
.Er EFAULT
instead and the transfer is cut short, which a client sees as a body shorter
than
.Ql Content-Length .
Either way the answer is never quietly wrong.
.It
Mount the archive directory read-only, and replace an archive by restarting.
The units ship with
.Ql Restart=on-failure .
.It
This is an availability property, not a confidentiality or integrity one:
whoever can truncate the archive already has write access to the data
directory and is outside the threat model.
.El
.Sh CONTAINERS
A distroless image — one static binary over
.Sy scratch ,
no shell, no package manager — is built from the repository
.Ql Containerfile
by
.Ql make container-build ,
and proven to serve by
.Ql make container-smoke ,
which runs it rootless and read-only under podman with
.Ql sandbox = require
set and asserts that both confinement layers report as applied inside
podman's own seccomp filter.
The two filters compose: what podman allows, cairnd's allowlist narrows.
.Pp
For systemd deployments, a rootless quadlet ships as
.Pa systemd/cairnd.container :
copy it to
.Pa ~/.config/containers/systemd/
and
.Ql systemctl --user daemon-reload .
It expects three mounts: the configuration at
.Pa ~/.config/cairn/cairn.conf ,
the archives under
.Pa ~/srv/zim ,
and a runtime directory for the socket, which systemd creates.
The socket appears on the runtime directory, so clients say
.Ql cairn -s "${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR}/cairn/cairn.sock" status .
The unit's process is podman rather than
.Nm ,
so the system unit's mount-based protections are deliberately not
repeated there; they are enforced at the container boundary instead,
and everything syscall-level is enforced by
.Nm
itself.
.Sh THE SOCKET DIRECTORY
A unix socket cannot be created in a directory that does not exist, and
.Nm
does not create one: it holds no write capability to any path.
Under systemd,
.Ql RuntimeDirectory=cairn
creates
.Pa /run/cairn
before the daemon starts and removes it after.
Running by hand, create the directory first or point
.Ql listen
somewhere that exists.
.Sh SIGNALS
.Nm
installs no handlers.
.Dv SIGTERM
and
.Dv SIGINT
terminate it, which is safe: nothing is buffered and nothing is written.
A unix socket left behind by a killed daemon is removed at the next start,
after a connect proves nothing is listening on it.
Under systemd,
.Ql RuntimeDirectory
cleans up instead.
.Sh LOGGING
One line per event on standard error, which systemd captures.
No entry content and nothing a client sent is ever logged, not even the
request target.
With
.Ql access_log = on ,
each request logs its method, status, body size and duration.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/var/lib/cairn/archives" -compact
.It Pa /etc/cairn/cairn.conf
Default configuration.
.It Pa /var/lib/cairn/archives
Default archive directory.
.It Pa /run/cairn/cairn.sock
Default socket.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
Exit status 1 covers a bad configuration, an archive that cannot be opened, a
socket that cannot be bound, and a sandbox that did not fully apply while
.Ql sandbox require
is set.
Exit status 2 is a usage error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Check a configuration and see what would be served:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# cairnd -c /etc/cairn/cairn.conf --check
.Ed
.Pp
Run in the foreground with confinement required:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# printf 'sandbox = require\enarchive_dir = /srv/zim\en' > /tmp/c.conf
# cairnd -c /tmp/c.conf
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cairn 1 ,
.Xr seccomp 2 ,
.Xr cairn.conf 5 ,
.Xr cairn-api 7 ,
.Xr landlock 7
.Sh CAVEATS
Pre-alpha, and unaudited.
There is no full-text search in cairn 1.x.
